{
  "result": "Every morning the city misplaced something small: a glove, a promise, the name of a street. Nobody worried. Cities were busy creatures, and a little forgetfulness seemed fair. But on Thursday the city misplaced its rain, and the clouds hung overhead looking embarrassed, unsure what to do with themselves next.\n\nMara, who repaired umbrellas beneath the railway bridge, noticed first. Her customers arrived carrying perfectly dry umbrellas and wearing expressions of vague disappointment. \u201cIt feels like we\u2019re waiting for someone,\u201d one man said. Mara nodded. She had felt that way since breakfast, when her kettle boiled without making steam.\n\nAt noon she climbed the tallest hill with a silver thimble in her pocket. Her grandmother had taught her that lost things preferred modest invitations. Shouting frightened them. Grand gestures made them suspicious. Mara set the thimble on a stone and whispered, \u201cThere\u2019s room here if you need somewhere safe.\u201d\n\nFor a while nothing happened. A beetle inspected her shoe. Below, trains stitched neighborhoods together. Then one drop fell into the thimble, so gently that Mara heard it only because she was listening. Another followed. The clouds shifted their heavy shoulders and finally remembered what they had come to do.\n\nBy evening gutters sang and windows glittered. Mara returned to her workshop soaked through, smiling. She placed the thimble beside her tools. Afterward, whenever anyone brought her a broken umbrella, she repaired it carefully, remembering that shelter was not always a roof. Sometimes it was simply being expected somewhere kindly.",
